Yeah, I think for this version of the project, you’re basically at a good stopping point.

You now have:

a working daily data collection pipeline
structured outputs
a reproducible process
enough automation to let it run without babysitting

And you’re right that the next real value probably comes from letting it collect data for a while, then analyzing patterns. Adding more models right now is probably not the best tradeoff if it mostly increases cost without changing the core edge.

Your idea about the screener is actually solid. The intuition is:

the biggest losers are not always the best mean reversion candidates
some of them are just experiencing true repricing
if your goal is only to capture something like +1% quickly, then what matters is not “did it fall a lot,” but:
how often similar drops bounce
how long the bounce takes
how often they keep bleeding instead

That suggests the project should shift from “find dramatic moves” to “find fast, statistically favorable rebounds.”

The big strategic direction

I think there are really 3 valuable branches from here:

1. Improve the screener

This is your example. Very good branch.

2. Build a labeling / backtesting layer

Turn each daily candidate into a measurable historical experiment.

3. Move from news explanation to tradeability prediction

Not “why did it fall?” but “is this the kind of fall that gives a clean +1% bounce in 1–5 days?”

That’s where the real edge would come from.

Strong ideas worth exploring
1. Recovery-time analysis by drop size

Exactly your idea, and I think this should be near the top.

For each stock/day in the last 1–3 years:

detect days with large negative moves
group by drop bucket, like:
3–5%
5–7%
7–10%
10%+
then compute:
probability of hitting +1% from entry
median days to hit +1%
probability of hitting +2%, +3%
max drawdown before recovery
percent that never recover within 5, 10, 20 trading days

This would directly answer whether extreme drops are actually bad for your style.

My guess:

moderate drops may be better than extreme drops
especially if your horizon is short
2. Separate news-driven drops from non-news drops

This one is very interesting.

Not all red days are equal:

some are broad market moves
some are sympathy moves
some are stock-specific headlines
some are earnings disasters
some are analyst downgrades
some are just overreaction to weak but not fatal news

You could classify drop days into categories like:

earnings miss
guidance cut
macro/sector move
legal/regulatory
AI/disruption fears
analyst downgrade
no obvious news

Then test which category mean reverts best.

This could be huge because:

maybe “analyst downgrade + 5% drop” mean reverts often
but “guidance cut + 10% drop” does not

That would make your pipeline actually useful for filtering.

3. Intraday reversal vs multi-day reversal

You currently seem to be thinking in daily bars and quick profit targets.

A very useful question is:

do these candidates bounce same day
next day
or only over a week+

For each event day, measure:

close-to-next-open
close-to-next-close
intraday low to close
open to close after event
days until +1%

Maybe the best opportunities are not “hold for several days,” but:

enter near close on oversold days
exit next morning if the bounce comes quickly

This could completely change the strategy style.

4. Oversold threshold conditioned on stock characteristics

A 6% drop in NVDA is not the same as a 6% drop in a small biotech.

You should test recovery behavior by stock type:

mega-cap tech
large-cap stable names
cyclicals
energy
healthcare/pharma
unprofitable high-beta names

Also by:

market cap
average volume
beta / realized volatility
ATR as percent of price

Maybe a 5% drop is extreme for one stock but normal noise for another.

That suggests a better screener might be:

rank by move relative to normal volatility
instead of pure percent drop

For example:

today’s drop / 20-day ATR
today’s return z-score vs past 60 days

This is probably one of the best improvements.

5. Gap-down versus grind-down distinction

Very important.

A stock that:

gaps down 8% at open on earnings

is very different from one that:

slowly sells off 8% throughout the day

You could split events into:

overnight gap-down
intraday collapse
gap-down then recover intraday
gap-down then continue selling

Then test which shape tends to mean revert faster.

This is strong because the chart pattern itself may contain more edge than the headline.

6. Relative move versus market/sector

A stock down 4% on a day where its sector is down 3.5% is different from a stock down 4% while market is flat.

Possible features:

stock return minus SPY return
stock return minus sector ETF return
abnormal return residual

Then test:

does mean reversion work better when the stock is an idiosyncratic loser
or when it is a sector sympathy loser

This seems very worth testing.

7. Detect “too bad to touch” events

This might be one of the most practical uses of your current LLM pipeline.

Instead of trying to find the best longs, build a filter that removes obvious traps.

Examples:

accounting fraud
secondary offering / dilution
dividend cut
regulatory disaster
catastrophic guidance
broken business model narrative
CEO departure in a fragile company
activist short report

Even if the model cannot find winners, it may be very useful in saying:

“this is not a mean reversion setup”

That alone improves expected value.

8. Time-to-profit distribution for your exact target

This one is very aligned with your stated goal.

You don’t care that much about long-term recovery.
You care about:

can I get +1%
how quickly
with what risk

So for each historical candidate:

assume entry rule
compute:
whether +1% was hit
how many days until hit
worst adverse move before hit
whether stop would have triggered first

That gives you a direct table like:

setup A: +1% hit rate 72%, median 2 days
setup B: +1% hit rate 41%, median 8 days
setup C: +1% hit rate 78%, median 1 day

This is way more relevant than “eventually recovered.”

9. Stop-loss feasibility study

If you want quick trades, you also need to know whether they are clean enough to risk-manage.

For each candidate, test simple rules:

target: +1%
stop: -2%
max hold: 3 days

Then see which event types or screen types survive that framework.

A strategy that wins eventually but regularly goes -6% first is probably not usable for your style.

10. Bounce quality score

You can eventually build a score from historical features.

Features could include:

drop size
volatility-adjusted drop
market-relative drop
sector-relative drop
news type
earnings flag
gap-down flag
intraday recovery flag
volume spike
prior trend
valuation / quality proxies

Then predict:

probability of +1% within 3 days
expected days to hit target
probability stop hits first

This is probably the eventual “real system.”

Some more offbeat but interesting ideas
11. “Second derivative” of sentiment

Not just whether news is bad, but whether:

price reaction was more negative than the actual news deserved

That is hard, but maybe your LLM pipeline can help label:

justified selloff
mild overreaction
severe overreaction

Then backtest whether “overreaction” labels bounce more.

12. Compare first shock day vs next-day continuation

Maybe buying the first big red day is bad.
Maybe buying the second day after stabilization is better.

You could test:

buy at day 0 close
buy at day 1 close if no new negative news
buy after first green close
buy after RSI / volatility reversal signal

That could matter a lot.

13. Ranking by “bounceability” instead of drop size

Instead of selecting the 12 worst daily movers, select the 12 highest:

abnormal-volume panic moves
but excluding catastrophic-news categories
and favoring liquid large caps with historically fast rebounds

This feels like a better endpoint than your current top-loser screen.

14. Use the LLM only where it adds the most value

You said cost is a concern. I agree.

A smart architecture could be:

use cheap quantitative filtering first on all names
send only top 3–5 ambiguous/high-potential names to the LLM
use LLM only for qualitative veto / trap detection

That gives you much better cost-efficiency.

What I think is most worth doing next

If I were organizing this into actual next experiments, I’d focus on these:

Tier 1
Historical recovery analysis by drop bucket
+1% target hit-rate / time-to-profit analysis
Volatility-adjusted screening instead of raw % drop
Tier 2
News category labeling
Gap-down vs grind-down behavior
Market-relative / sector-relative filtering
Tier 3
Build a bounceability score
Use LLM only as a veto layer
Test entry timing variants
My biggest meta-thought

Right now the project is framed as:

“find the biggest losers and understand why”

But the better framing may be:

“find selloffs that are statistically likely to give me a fast, low-friction +1% bounce”

That sounds similar, but it changes everything:

universe selection
features
labels
holding period
role of news
usefulness of the LLM

Your edge probably does not come from explaining drops.
It probably comes from identifying:

which kinds of drops are safe to fade
and which are value traps / regime shifts

That is a much stronger grand-scheme direction.

